nd scoped informal statements. The definitions use actual Real.sin, Real.cos, Real.sqrt, Real.pi and natural-number indexing; the derivative declarations use HasDerivAt and deriv. The finite sum ranges over j=0,...,n-1 with summand sin((j+1)t)^2. At n=0 the sum and Wronskian are zero, so the unrestricted algebraic identities remain valid; strict negativity correctly requires n>0 and 0<x<1. The j=1 sine-square term proves positivity on that interval. Endpoint strict negativity of W is not asserted. At n=2,x=1/2 the actual Wronskian is -4*pi, whereas the old expression is -6*pi. The endpoint coefficient expands to -2*pi^4*(4*n^3+6*n^2+4*n+1), which is negative for every natural n, including zero; this establishes coefficient algebra, not a remainder estimate. In natural language, the ten-part root asserts the mode derivative, Wronskian product-to-sum identity, finite sine-square identity, conditional strict Wronskian negativity, the exact midpoint value and old-formula inequivalence, endpoint coefficient identity and negativity, conditional mirrored-gap identity, and nonzero-jump stationarity equivalence. The detailed binder-preserving readback appears below. For the FH identification, jump=rho_left-rho_right. Reversing both jump and velocity at the right interface makes its contribution have the same sign as the left contribution; equality of squared values then gives the factor two. Subtracting lower from upper contributions gives 2*jump*gap_switch. These are satisfiable, explicit scalar hypotheses; no positivity or eigenvalue ordering is needed for this algebra. The stationarity hypothesis jump!=0 is essential: when jump=0, the product vanishes for arbitrary f. The old mirror-factor counterexample evaluates to 18!=9. No hidden premise, vacuous hypothesis or semantic inflation was found within the declared local scope.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R7-formal-independent-runtime\",\n      \"verdict\": \"APPROVED\",\n      \"reason\": \"All 144 listed snapshot has